# Fixed Flat Charge Calculation Flow

## 1) Purpose
This document explains the charge-calculation path implemented by `FixedFlatChargeMethod` for the Production Engineering team presentation.

### Highlights
- rule derivation that selects the billing rule before calculation
- product / CRM resolution that maps the rule to the final billing setup
- multi-method charge aggregation when multiple billing products are returned
- how FX is used to normalize the charge-on amount before the final charge is computed
- the fixed-flat calculator path only; other strategies are only referenced as context

Scope:
- only the fixed-flat strategy
- only the calculation path after the billing inputs have already been resolved
- includes the conditions, DB table sources, calculation formulas, and FX call behavior

Out of scope:
- rule derivation details beyond the summary view
- product/CRM resolution details beyond the summary view
- full multi-method aggregation implementation details
- other calculators such as tiered or flat-rate strategies

> Note: calculators such as tiered or flat-rate strategies are out of scope for this SG presentation and are only mentioned to show where `FixedFlatChargeMethod` fits in the broader charge engine.

---

## 2) Where the data comes from
`FixedFlatChargeMethod` does **not** query the database directly. It receives DTOs that are populated upstream from these DB tables:

| DB table | DTO used in this method | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `CHRG_BILLING_PRODUCT` | `BillingProductDto` | Supplies the charge mode, value, rate, min/max, and currency |
| `CHRG_CHARGE_METHOD` | `ChargeMethodDto` | Supplies `chargeOn1` (for example `Amount` or `Txn_Count`) |
| `CHRG_CHARGE_CODE` | `ChargeCodeDto` | Identifies the charge code context passed into the calculator |

The runtime input to the method is:
- `Charges request`
- `BillingProductDto billingProduct`
- `ChargeCodeDto chargeCode`
- `ChargeMethodDto chargeMethod`

### 2.1 How rule derivation and product/CRM resolution work at a high level
1. Rule derivation picks the applicable business rule from `CHRG_RULES` using the request context.
2. Product resolution maps that rule to a product code from `CHRG_PRODUCT`.
3. CRM resolution maps the product and customer context to the correct setup from `CHRG_CRM_SETUP`.
4. Billing-product resolution returns one or more matching billing products from `CHRG_BILLING_PRODUCT`.
5. Each billing product is then processed independently by the calculator.

---

## 3) End-to-end flowchart

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Client sends charge request"] --> B["System prepares the charge request context"]
    B --> C["Rule derivation from CHRG_RULES"]
    C --> D["Matched Rule"]
    D --> E["Product resolution from CHRG_PRODUCT"]
    E --> F["Resolved Product Code"]
    F --> G["CRM setup resolution from CHRG_CRM_SETUP"]
    G --> H["Resolved CRM Setup"]
    H --> I["Billing product derivation from CHRG_BILLING_PRODUCT"]
    I --> J{"One or more billing products returned?"}
    J -->|No| K["No billing product found - upstream error path"]
    J -->|Yes| L["Iterate billing products one by one"]

    L --> M["Load billing product, charge setup, and charge code details"]
    M --> N{"Charge calculation type"}
    N -->|Fixed Flat| O["Run Fixed Flat charge calculation"]
    N -->|Tiered / Flat Rate / Other| P["Other charge calculation path - out of scope for this SG presentation"]

    O --> Q["Start charge calculation"]
    Q --> R["Determine the basis for charge calculation"]

    R --> S{"Charge based on"}
    S -->|Transaction Amount| T["Read transaction amount in the original currency"]
    T --> U{"Does the transaction currency match the charge currency?"}
    U -->|Yes| V["Use the amount as-is"]
    U -->|No| W["Convert the amount using FX"]
    W --> X["Use the converted amount as the charge basis"]

    S -->|Transaction Count| Y["Use one transaction as the charge basis"]
    S -->|Other| Z["Unsupported charge basis - stop calculation"]

    V --> AA["Select the charge calculation mode"]
    X --> AA
    Y --> AA

    AA --> AB{"Per unit charge?"}
    AB -->|Yes| AC["Calculate charge per unit"]
    AB -->|No| AD{"Percentage charge?"}
    AD -->|Yes| AE["Calculate charge as a percentage"]
    AD -->|No| AF{"Fixed flat charge?"}
    AF -->|Yes| AG["Apply the fixed flat amount"]
    AF -->|No| AH{"Per million charge?"}
    AH -->|Yes| AI["Calculate charge per million"]
    AH -->|No| AJ["No valid calculation mode matched - charge remains zero"]

    AC --> AK["Apply minimum and maximum limits"]
    AE --> AK
    AG --> AK
    AI --> AK
    AJ --> AK

    AK --> AL["Compare the charge against allowed limits"]
    AL --> AM{"Is the charge amount valid?"}
    AM -->|Yes| AN["Format the final charge amount in the target currency"]
    AM -->|No| AO["Skip final formatting"]

    AN --> AP["Store the result for this billing product"]
    AO --> AP
    AP --> AQ{"More billing products?"}
    AQ -->|Yes| L
    AQ -->|No| AR["Add all billing-product charges to get the final charge amount"]
    AR --> AS["Return final response"]
    Z --> AT["Return calculation error"]
```

---

## 4) Calculation conditions

### 4.1 Charge-on selection
`deriveChargeOn()` supports only these values:

| chargeOn1 | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `Amount` | Convert or reuse the transaction amount and use it as `chargeOnValue` |
| `Txn_Count` | Use `BigDecimal.ONE` as `chargeOnValue` |
| any other value | Raise `UNSUPPORTED_CHARGE_ON` |

### 4.2 Computation mode selection
The selected calculation branch depends on the billing-product flags:

| Mode | Condition | Helper used |
|---|---|---|
| Absolute per unit | `BillingUtils.isComputeByAbsolutePerUnit(...)` | `ChargeUtils.doComputeByAbsoluteUnit(...)` |
| Rate | `BillingUtils.isComputeByRate(...)` | `ChargeUtils.doComputeByRate(...)` |
| Absolute flat | `BillingUtils.isComputeByAbsoluteFlat(...)` | `ChargeUtils.doComputeByAbsoluteFlat(...)` |
| Per million | `BillingUtils.isComputeByPerMillion(...)` | `ChargeUtils.doComputeByPerMillion(...)` |
| No match | none of the above | amount remains `ZERO` |

### 4.3 Output adjustment
After formula execution the method always applies:
1. min/max clamp via `ChargeUtils.doCompareMinAndMaxChargeAmount(...)`
2. currency formatting via `getFormattedCurrency(...)` when the result is non-negative

---

## 5) Calculation formulas

| Branch | Formula |
|---|---|
| Absolute per unit | `chargeOnValue * chargeValue` |
| Rate | `(chargeOnValue * chargeValue) / 100` |
| Absolute flat | `chargeValue` |
| Per million | `(chargeOnValue * chargeValue) / 1,000,000` |

### 5.1 Charge-rate adjustment
When the helper method supports `chargeRate`, the rate flag may change the computed amount before the final clamp/format step.

### 5.2 Min/max clamp
If the billing product defines a minimum or maximum, the computed value is clamped into the allowed range before being returned.

### 5.3 Currency formatting
The final amount is formatted using the project’s standard scale and rounding rules.

---

## 6) FX call behavior

The FX hook is triggered only inside `deriveChargeOn()` when `chargeOn1 == Amount`.

### Decision rule
- If the source currency equals the billing-product charge currency, the method uses the amount directly.
- If the currencies differ, the method calls `doFxConversion(...)` from the shared calculator base class.

### FX call details
`ChargeCalculationAbstract.doFxConversion(...)`:
- checks whether conversion is actually required
- resolves the FX service from the configured rules source
- invokes `FXService.doFxConversion(...)`
- logs FX observability metadata

### Practical effect
The fixed-flat calculator only needs the FX result as the `chargeOnValue`; it does not implement currency conversion itself.

---

## 7) Error and fallback behavior

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| Unsupported `chargeOn1` | `GbpsProcessingException(UNSUPPORTED_CHARGE_ON)` |
| Unexpected exception during compute | wrapped as `GbpsProcessingException(CHARGE_COMPUTATION_FAILED)` |
| No computation mode matched | amount remains zero and the method continues through clamp/format steps |
| Negative computed amount | formatting branch is skipped |

---

## 8) Multi-billing-product handling

Sometimes the upstream resolution layer returns a list of billing products instead of a single billing product.

### How it is managed
- The service layer iterates through the list one billing product at a time.
- Each billing product is routed through the selected calculator independently.
- The per-product charge results are then aggregated into the final response.

### Why this is needed
- One rule can map to more than one valid billing product.
- Different billing products can carry different charge values, rates, currencies, or min/max limits.
- Processing them separately keeps the charge logic deterministic and auditable.

### Why FX is used
FX is required when the amount used for calculation is in a different currency from the billing-product charge currency.

In practical terms:
- the incoming transaction amount may be in one currency
- the charge configuration may expect another currency
- FX converts or normalizes the amount so the calculator works with a consistent `chargeOnValue`
- this ensures the final charge amount is derived in the correct charge currency before formatting and return

---

## 9) Execution summary

1. Read the upstream billing inputs from DTOs.
2. Derive `chargeOnValue` from `Amount` or `Txn_Count`.
3. Convert via FX only when the charge-on amount currency differs from the billing product currency.
4. Pick the fixed-flat formula based on the billing-product flags.
5. Clamp the result to min/max bounds.
6. Format and return the final charge amount.

---

## 10) Short reference for reviewers

- Main class: `src/main/java/com/sc/csl/retail/charges/factory/FixedFlatChargeMethod.java`
- Shared FX logic: `src/main/java/com/sc/csl/retail/charges/factory/ChargeCalculationAbstract.java`
- Upstream DB tables: `CHRG_BILLING_PRODUCT`, `CHRG_CHARGE_METHOD`, `CHRG_CHARGE_CODE`





